00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
A three-year experiment shows aspen trees retain chemical memories of past droughts in their leaves. This is Planetterrian Daily Episode 209. University of Utah biologist tracked aspen responses across multiple dry periods. They observed chemical shifts in leaf composition that persisted after the drought ended. These changes constitute functional memories that may prepare the trees for future water stress. The record appears in annual growth rings and extends into leaf chemistry. The experiment followed the same

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.260
trees through successive seasons to separate memory signals from annual variation.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.580
Most people assume trees respond only to the weather happening right now, yet a three-year

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:43.980
experiment demonstrates that aspens maintain functional memories of earlier droughts

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.280
through lasting changes in leaf chemistry.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.220
The study recorded these chemical shifts in the same individuals across successive years,

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.020
showing the memory is stored locally rather than reset each spring.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:01:00.140
One concrete detail is the three-year span of repeated measurements that separated the

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.480
memory signal from simple seasonal variation.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.740
This stored information appears to prepare the tree for faster or more efficient responses

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.080
if dry conditions return.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:14.420
Researchers can now examine whether similar leaf level records exist in other deciduous

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.520
species that face repeated water stress.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.220
The immediate next measurements will check whether trees showing stronger chemical memories

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.800
maintain higher survival rates through additional drought cycles.

